---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/indas/indas10.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope & Applicability" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Adjusting Events" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Non-Adjusting Events" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Dividends Declared After the Reporting Period" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Going Concern Assessment" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Indian Board Meeting & AGM Timeline Context" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Date of Authorisation for Issue" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Ind AS 10 vs IAS 10 – Full Comparison" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- ============ SECTION 1 – CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK LINK ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci">
        <div class="ctitle ci">Identical adjusting/non-adjusting distinction to IAS 10</div>
        <div class="principle">Ind AS 10 applies the IDENTICAL principle as IAS 10 – events after the reporting period are classified as ADJUSTING (providing evidence of conditions existing AT the reporting date) or NON-ADJUSTING (indicative of conditions arising AFTER the reporting date).</div>
        <div class="elabel">HOW IND AS 10 MAPS TO THE FRAMEWORK</div>
        <div class="check-list"><span><strong>Faithful representation</strong> – only conditions existing at the reporting date should adjust the recognised amounts – identical to IAS 10</span><span><strong>Timeliness vs relevance trade-off</strong> – significant non-adjusting events are disclosed rather than recognised, balancing timely reporting with relevance – identical to IAS 10</span></div>
        <div class="note">Ind AS 10 introduced NO substantive change to this framework relative to IAS 10 – its Indian practical relevance concerns the specific timeline of board meeting approval and AGM processes under the Companies Act, 2013.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cs">
        <div class="ctitle cs">The dividend treatment – identical to IAS 10</div>
        <div class="formula-box"><div class="f-eq">Dividends declared AFTER the reporting date = NON-ADJUSTING event → NOT recognised as a liability at the reporting date</div></div>
        <div class="callout info callout--flush">IDENTICAL to IAS 10 – a genuinely important rule, as many jurisdictions' PRIOR local GAAP frameworks (including old Indian GAAP) required dividend liability recognition at the reporting date, a practice Ind AS 10/IAS 10 both explicitly REJECT.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 2 – SCOPE & DEFINITIONS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope &amp; Applicability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw"><div class="ctitle cw">What Ind AS 10 covers</div><div class="principle">Ind AS 10 applies to the accounting/disclosure of events between the reporting date and the date financial statements are AUTHORISED FOR ISSUE – IDENTICAL scope to IAS 10.</div></div>
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Key definitions you must know</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Adjusting event</div><div class="glossary-def">IDENTICAL definition to IAS 10 – provides evidence of conditions existing at the reporting date.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Date of authorisation for issue</div><div class="glossary-def">The date the board of directors (or equivalent) approves financial statements for issue – a key reference date, identical concept to IAS 10.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 3 – ADJUSTING EVENTS (worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Adjusting Events (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical examples/treatment to IAS 10</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example 1: Customer insolvency confirming a receivable impairment</div><div class="wk-body">An Indian company's major customer, already showing signs of financial distress before the March 31 year-end, is declared insolvent by the NCLT (National Company Law Tribunal) in May, before the financial statements are authorised for issue in June.</div><div class="wk-answer">This is an ADJUSTING event – the insolvency confirms a condition (customer's deteriorating financial position) that EXISTED at the reporting date, requiring the receivable to be WRITTEN DOWN in the March 31 financial statements – an IDENTICAL treatment to how IAS 10 would classify the same fact pattern globally.</div></div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 4 – NON-ADJUSTING EVENTS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Non-Adjusting Events</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Identical treatment to IAS 10</div><div class="text-supporting">Events indicative of conditions arising AFTER the reporting date (e.g. a major acquisition, a natural disaster affecting facilities, significant share price declines) are NOT recognised but DISCLOSED if material – IDENTICAL to IAS 10.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 5 – DIVIDENDS DECLARED AFTER THE REPORTING PERIOD ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Dividends Declared After the Reporting Period</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical to IAS 10</div><div class="text-supporting">Dividends proposed/declared AFTER the reporting date (even if recommended by the board before authorisation for issue) are NOT recognised as a liability at the reporting date – disclosed instead in the notes – IDENTICAL to IAS 10.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 6 – GOING CONCERN ASSESSMENT ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Going Concern Assessment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Identical to IAS 10</div><div class="text-supporting">If management determines AFTER the reporting date that it intends to liquidate the entity or cease trading, or has no realistic alternative, financial statements are NOT prepared on a going concern basis – IDENTICAL to IAS 10, this determination itself functions as an ADJUSTING event regardless of when the intention arose.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 7 – INDIAN BOARD MEETING & AGM TIMELINE CONTEXT (worked example) ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Indian Board Meeting &amp; AGM Timeline Context (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A practical Indian corporate governance timing consideration</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Under the Companies Act, 2013, Indian companies typically hold a board meeting to APPROVE financial statements before their AGM (Annual General Meeting), with dividends often RECOMMENDED by the board but only formally DECLARED upon shareholder approval at the AGM – occurring well AFTER the reporting date and even after the date of board approval. This reinforces why Ind AS 10's non-adjusting dividend treatment is particularly significant in the Indian corporate governance context.</div>
      <div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example 2: Board-recommended dividend timing</div><div class="wk-body">An Indian listed company's board RECOMMENDS a final dividend of ₹5 per share on May 20 (after the March 31 year-end) when approving financial statements for issue, with shareholder approval expected at the AGM in August.</div><div class="wk-answer">The recommended dividend is NOT recognised as a liability in the March 31 financial statements – it is a NON-ADJUSTING event, disclosed in the notes, with formal declaration only occurring at the AGM – an IDENTICAL treatment to how IAS 10 would classify a comparable board-recommended dividend globally, reflecting the Indian corporate governance sequence.</div></div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 8 – DATE OF AUTHORISATION FOR ISSUE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Date of Authorisation for Issue</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Identical to IAS 10</div><div class="text-supporting">The date of authorisation for issue is typically the date of BOARD APPROVAL (not the AGM date) – IDENTICAL concept to IAS 10, marking the cut-off point for identifying subsequent events requiring evaluation.</div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 9 – DISCLOSURE ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Identical disclosure requirements to IAS 10</div><div class="check-list"><span>Date of authorisation for issue and who authorised it</span><span>Nature and estimated financial effect of material non-adjusting events</span><span>Dividends proposed/declared after the reporting date but before authorisation</span></div></div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 10 – JUDGMENTS & ESTIMATES ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where management judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Classifying an event as adjusting vs non-adjusting</span><span>Determining materiality for non-adjusting event disclosure</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Estimated financial effect of material non-adjusting events, where estimable</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="callout warn callout--warn-flush">A common practical error is treating a BOARD-RECOMMENDED dividend as a liability at the reporting date – Ind AS 10 (like IAS 10) explicitly prohibits this, a genuinely important transition point from older Indian GAAP practice.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 11 – IND AS 10 vs IAS 10 ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Ind AS 10 vs IAS 10 – Full Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comprehensive comparison table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>IAS 10</span><span>Ind AS 10</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Adjusting/non-adjusting classification, dividend treatment</span><span>Standard model</span><span>IDENTICAL – no substantive divergence</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Board approval / AGM sequence</span><span>Varies by jurisdiction's corporate governance framework</span><span>Companies Act, 2013's board-then-AGM sequence reinforces the non-adjusting dividend treatment's practical significance</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Ind AS 10 is essentially IDENTICAL to IAS 10 in substance – its Indian practical relevance is confined to the corporate governance TIMELINE context, not any divergence in the underlying accounting principles.</div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 12 – STATEMENT MAPPING ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid"><div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Comprehensive event-to-statement mapping table</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / transaction</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Adjusting event (e.g. receivable write-down)</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Recognised in the reporting period financial statements</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Non-adjusting event (e.g. proposed dividend)</span><span><span class="badge bp">Notes</span></span><span>Disclosed only, not recognised</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Post-year-end going concern determination</span><span><span class="badge bs">Balance Sheet</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Fundamental basis-of-preparation change, treated as adjusting</span></div>
      </div>
    </div></div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 13 – RELATED STANDARDS ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
        <div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>IAS 10</span><span>The global counterpart standard, near-identically converged</span></div>
          <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 1</span><span>Requires the going concern basis disclosure interacting with Ind AS 10's assessment</span></div>
          <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>Ind AS 37</span><span>Governs provisions/contingent liabilities that may be confirmed by adjusting events</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card cn"><div class="ctitle cn">Quick-reference glossary</div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">NCLT</div><div class="glossary-def">National Company Law Tribunal – India's adjudicating authority for insolvency/company law matters, often the trigger for adjusting event determinations.</div></div>
        <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">AGM</div><div class="glossary-def">Annual General Meeting – where Indian shareholders formally approve/declare dividends recommended by the board.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ SECTION 14 – GLOSSARY ============ -->
    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <Glossary entries={[
      { term: "Adjusting event", definition: "Provides evidence of conditions existing at the reporting date – identical to IAS 10." },
      { term: "Date of authorisation for issue", definition: "The date the board of directors (or equivalent) approves financial statements for issue – a key reference date, identical concept to IAS 10." },
      { term: "NCLT", definition: "National Company Law Tribunal – India's adjudicating authority for insolvency/company law matters, often the trigger for adjusting event determinations." },
      { term: "AGM", definition: "Annual General Meeting – where Indian shareholders formally approve/declare dividends recommended by the board." },
    ]} />

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
